  "query_preview": "..As September winds toward a close, the pattern\nremains quite active. Two major hurricanes, category 4 Hurricane\nOdalys and category 5 Hurricane Polo, remain across the eastern\nPacific. A strong Pacific trough continues to push into the\nPacific Northwest, while a strong upper level ridge remains\ncentered near the Gulf Coast. The interplay between this Pacific\ntrough and the two Hurricanes is the biggest forecast challenge\nfor the next 7 days. \n\nCurrent National Hurricane Center track forecasts bring Hurricane\nPolo into Baja Sur Monday, whereas Odalys is forecast to initially\nmove to the north before weakening and shifting to the south and\neast well off the Mexican coast. Integral to how these hurricanes\nmay bring heavy rain to the Great Basin is the exact track and \ntiming of the upper level Pacific trough and the tracks of these \nhurricanes...and how they interplay to advect moisture into the \nSouthwest. \n\nA portion of the guidance envelope would suggest deep tropical\nmoisture from the further west storm (Odalys) would advect north\nand east into the Great Basin ahead of the upper level trough,\nbringing a period of moderate to heavy rain Monday into Tuesday\nmorning across southern and eastern Utah. \n\nAnother portion of the guidance envelope would have the trough in\na slow enough position to pull some portion of the tropical\nmoisture from the plume associated with Polo and again, bring a\nperiod of moderate to heavy rain to southern and eastern Utah\nMonday into early Tuesday.\n\nAround 25% of ensemble members from the 00Z runs, however, \nlargely rejects any tropical influx of moisture ahead of the \nPacific trough and just brings a typical strong cold front and\nassociated period of precipitation through the state. \n\nThe contrast between the tropical moisture plume favored ensemble\nmembers and the upper level trough only ensemble members is wide\nfor precipitation. For those with a strong tropical moisture\nplume, the 25th/75th percentile QPF ranges are around 0.50-1.50\ninches of rain across a broad area of southern and eastern Utah,\nwith locally higher amounts up to 2 inches. For those without,\nUtah is looking at more of a general 0.25-0.50 inch event. \n\nThese types of events are notoriously difficult to capture for\nmost ensemble members, when the interplay of Hurricane tracks\nacross the eastern Pacific and a deepening Pacific trough will be\nthe main driver of the forecast. Subtle differences in tracks,\nspeed and depth of the trough can cause big differences in\nexpected precipitation, particularly at the higher end of the\nranges. \n\nFor now, will continue to message the potential for an extended\nperiod of moderate to heavy rain across southern and eastern Utah\nMonday, but will not issue any flood watches yet. This is\nsomething to monitor moving forward. Those with potential outdoor\nplans on Monday in these areas should consider alternate plans\noutside of slot canyons, normally dry washes, and other flood\nprone areas. Those near recent burn scars such as Widemouth 2,\nCottonwood, Forsyth, among others should continue to monitor\nforecasts.",
